        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The operand defines the fluid density, and the mtrl variable would for instance be the hydraulic conductivity. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="source/includes/integration/pde_operators/numerical/NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV.h" line="20" column="5" bodyfile="source/modules/integration/pde_operators/numerical_integration/NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV.cpp" bodystart="18" bodyend="51"/>
      </memberdef>
      <memberdef kind="function" id="classcsmp_1_1_num_integral___n_t__mixed__op__d_ni__d_v_1acc4182be3a730bfd5a41a4f671c1d1c2" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" final="yes" virt="virtual">
        <type>void</type>
        <definition>void csmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV&lt; dim, CELL &gt;::GetOperands</definition>
        <argsstring>(const CELL&lt; dim &gt; &amp;) override final</argsstring>
        <name>GetOperands</name>
        <qualifiedname>csmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV::GetOperands</qualifiedname>
        <reimplements refid="classcsmp_1_1_math_operator_r_h_s_1ad5614fa4de569146a684d28635eb9ff7">GetOperands</reimplements>
        <param>
          <type>const CELL&lt; dim &gt; &amp;</type>
          <defname>e</defname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Reads the <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_operand" kindref="compound">Operand</ref> from either the element or the nodes. Reads the material property from the element, and its multipliers from the nodes for later interpolation to the integration points. <linebreak/>
</para>
<para>If the MathOperator is used in a transient calculation, the function <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_num_integral___n_t__mixed__op__d_ni__d_v_1a1ab645b3090a033f910108f5d2b32168" kindref="member">MaterialPropertyTimeMultiplier()</ref> allows to multiply the material parameter with the time-icrement to achieve symmetry with the lefthand side of the equation. This is done, for instance, if the <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_math_operator_r_h_s" kindref="compound">MathOperatorRHS</ref> is used to compute the hydrostatic pressure contribution to a transient system. Do not use MultiplyWithTimeIncrement() in this case, since the acceleration of gravity must not be multiplied with delta t. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="source/includes/integration/pde_operators/numerical/NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV.h" line="26" column="10" bodyfile="source/modules/integration/pde_operators/numerical_integration/NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV.cpp" bodystart="92" bodyend="116"/>
      </memberdef>
      <memberdef kind="function" id="classcsmp_1_1_num_integral___n_t__mixed__op__d_ni__d_v_1ade00165e4c1f5c1e5f5797b5b0c7c07f" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" final="yes" virt="virtual">
        <type>void</type>
        <definition>void csmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV&lt; dim, CELL &gt;::ComputeContribution</definition>
        <argsstring>(const CELL&lt; dim &gt; &amp;) override final</argsstring>
        <name>ComputeContribution</name>
        <qualifiedname>csmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV::ComputeContribution</qualifiedname>
        <reimplements refid="classcsmp_1_1_math_operator_r_h_s_1ab77b3c1f4f078290ec9330a916482ed7">ComputeContribution</reimplements>
        <param>
          <type>const CELL&lt; dim &gt; &amp;</type>
          <defname>e</defname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Computes, for example the body force term ensuing from the density field within the element. <linebreak/>
</para>
<para>This involves numerical integration over the element volume of the product:</para>
<para>mtrl * volume integral (NT nmult NT op) dNi</para>
<para>Here, the op (<ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_operand" kindref="compound">Operand</ref>) is the fluid density and &apos;nmult&apos; is the nodal property which is used as material multiplier. <linebreak/>
</para>
<sect1 id="group___c_s_m_pglobal_functions_1arguments">
<title>Input Arguments</title><para>A reference to the finite-element from which the contribution is computed. </para>
</sect1>
        </detaileddescription>
